WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.132
Linux security is the practice of operating Linux systems so required work can happen while unnecessary risk is reduced.

2
00:00:08.132 --> 00:00:18.403
The goal is not to make a server impossible to use, or to turn every workstation into a locked box with no business value.

3
00:00:18.403 --> 00:00:28.675
The goal is reliable operation with controlled exposure: the right users can do the right work, services run for a clear reason, software is

4
00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:33.811
maintained, activity is visible, and recovery is possible when something goes wrong.

5
00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:35.951
Linux appears in many places.

6
00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:46.222
It may run a web server, a cloud instance, a container host, a developer workstation, a network appliance, an embedded device, or a small

7
00:00:46.222 --> 00:00:47.078
homelab system.

8
00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:51.786
Those environments have different risk levels, but the fundamentals are familiar.

9
00:00:51.786 --> 00:01:02.058
Security depends on installation choices, configuration, accounts, file permissions, packages, services, SSH access, firewall rules, logs, backups, and the way changes are approved and

10
00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:02.486
validated.

11
00:01:02.486 --> 00:01:05.053
Least privilege is a central theme.

12
00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:09.761
A user should not have more access than the role requires.

13
00:01:09.761 --> 00:01:14.897
A service should not run if nobody owns it or needs it.

14
00:01:14.897 --> 00:01:20.033
A port should not be reachable just because a package opened it.

15
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:26.025
A file mode should match the purpose of the file, not convenience during setup.

16
00:01:26.025 --> 00:01:30.732
These decisions are small, but they compound into a security posture.

17
00:01:30.732 --> 00:01:34.584
Linux is powerful because administrators can change almost anything.

18
00:01:34.584 --> 00:01:44.856
That power requires discipline: inventory first, test changes, use a change window for important systems, document assumptions, keep rollback options, monitor results, and maintain

19
00:01:44.856 --> 00:01:45.284
backups.

20
00:01:45.284 --> 00:01:50.848
Secure operation is a lifecycle, not a single command, tool, or hardening checklist.
